#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074706} :activity/id #uuid "d488bf85-6fde-58c0-8209-525fccfed6ea" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074706 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075061 :tutorial/content "The systems of equations we've encountered up to now have all had *exactly one solution*.\n\nFor example, consider the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2x + 1 \\\\ y &=1 - x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUsing either substitution or elimination, we can show that the solution $(x,y)$ to this system is $(0,1)$. This is the *only* solution, and it tells us the point of intersection between the two corresponding lines.\n\n![](images/bd/bd7edb530e16b853318a3e38785c7b6353c1b54f10ee3c4e2de7da915450f6d9.png)\n\nIn general, systems of equations can have\n\n- *exactly one* solution (like the example above)\n- *infinitely many* solutions\n- *no solutions*\n\nTo determine the number of solutions to a system of equations, we solve it using either substitution or elimination and interpret the result.\n\nLet's look at a system of equations with infinitely many solutions." :tutorial/id #uuid "35007d3d-ea87-5457-99a5-6d107cd45994" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9683 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0faaf6cd-b684-5899-a9ba-b74810f5cd7f" :step/math-academy-id 229007 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074707}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074707 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075063 :tutorial/content "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x - y &=-2 \\\\ 6x - 2y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo show that this system has infinitely many solutions, we solve the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we get a $-6x$ term that can cancel with the $6x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-y &=-2 && \\text{multiply by } -2 \\\\ 6x-2y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x + 2y &= 4 \\\\\n6x - 2y &= -4 \\\\\n0 &= (4 - 4) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=0$ is *always* true. This implies that this system of equations has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nGeometrically, this means that the equations\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}3x - y =-2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}6x -2y=-4}\n$$\n\nboth represent the *same straight line*. In other words, the two lines **coincide**.\n\n![](images/e9/e9e98008aa5972889efdd3af0689da310c69bca06dd410a391a37e22aa8b0098.png)\n\nThese two lines intersect at an infinite number of points. The system has infinitely many solutions because there are infinitely many intersection points.\n\nAnother way to see that this system has infinitely many solutions is to notice that the lines are nonzero multiples of each other.\n\nFor example, if we multiply the equation of the first line by $2$, we get the second line!\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-y &=-2 \\\\ 2\\cdot(3x-y) &=2\\cdot(-2) \\\\ 6x-2y &=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now look at an example of a system with *no solutions*." :tutorial/id #uuid "e1a6bcd8-b266-562d-b3d4-291e2671d40e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9685 :tutorial/title "A System With Infinitely Many Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "73dd188e-65f8-5e92-8177-3b0d533157d0" :step/math-academy-id 229008 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074708}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074708 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073776 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073480} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a24fb4f3-6e3c-5c45-b31e-85b7ce290eda" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050190}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529332} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529320}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Solutions to a System"} :step/id #uuid "1ed2bf12-756f-5fad-b1d5-51e295f52ff5" :step/math-academy-id 229009 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074709}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074709 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075049 :tutorial/content "As we've seen, the solutions of a linear system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}a_{1}x + b_{1}y &=c_{1} \\\\ a_{2}x + b_{2}y &=c_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncorrespond to the points of intersection of the two lines. There are three possibilities:\n\n| ![](images/a1/a1e58afecd7b919f6fd77b48f7bcb7219e54d2f86f8ef07df619505a4643bdd4.png) | ![](images/a4/a466524eb95d85e6c383a37955fffbf02fc9391b0b09a85fce7f44ab934bdc91.png) | ![](images/c7/c73a9f1bfbdd4efa1826b77a7bce1a9e1cbb13e61d718ffe18404e13a31a34f4.png) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n\n- Case $(a)$ shows two parallel lines. Because there are no points of intersection, the system has *no solutions*. This happens whenever the lines have the *same* slope but *different* $y$-intercepts.\n- Case $(b)$ shows two lines intersecting at a single point. Because there is a single point of intersection, the system has *one solution*.\n- Case $(c)$ shows two lines that coincide. Because there are infinitely many points of intersection, the system has *infinitely many* solutions. This happens whenever the lines have the *same* slope and *same* $y$-intercept. In such cases, the two lines are nonzero multiples of each other." :tutorial/id #uuid "a5bf756b-971d-599f-975a-198fe3d0bf9b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8708 :tutorial/title "The Intersection of Two Lines"} :step/id #uuid "dbf0a55c-efc2-582d-992a-f9a87ea60087" :step/math-academy-id 229013 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074710}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074710 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073777 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073481} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fab4792-0fe1-5a5c-8754-509af469da68" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050245}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561267} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561266}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Intersection Points of Two Lines"} :step/id #uuid "383e278d-a147-5443-aeae-8cf7659f6442" :step/math-academy-id 229014 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074711}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075062 :tutorial/content "When a system of equations has one solution, we can describe the solution as a single point $(x,y)$. This represents the point where the two lines intersect.\n\nNow, suppose a system of equations has *infinitely many* solutions. Can we describe the solutions to the system in a similar way?\n\nIndeed, we can. To demonstrate, consider the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x - y &=-2 \\\\ 6x - 2y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nEarlier, we showed that this system has infinitely many solutions. Therefore, the corresponding lines coincide.\n\nOur goal is to express *all* the solutions of this system in the form $({\\color{blue}{x}},{\\color{red}{y}})$. To do this, we find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the equations for $y$. It doesn't matter which, so let's pick the first equation.\n\n$$\n3x - y =-2⇒y = 3x + 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, for any given $x$-value, the corresponding $y$-value is\n\n$$\ny = 3x+2\n$$\n\nFinally, we can express the intersection points as\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}{x}},{\\color{red}{y}}) = \\left({\\color{blue}{x}},{\\color{red}{3x+2}}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}x$ is *any* number." :tutorial/id #uuid "6b209a68-8c6f-5ad2-877d-3224be966d73" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9684 :tutorial/title "Describing General Solutions of Systems With Infinitely Many Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "1feffb0c-16a2-5e89-b733-4597b8c07965" :step/math-academy-id 229017 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074712}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074712 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075064 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to our system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x - y &=-2 \\\\ 6x - 2y &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe showed that the set of intersection points $(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x,y) = \\left(x,3x + 2\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number.\n\nThis formula allows us to generate as many intersection points as we want! To do this, we select *any* $x$-value and substitute it into the formula above.\n\nFor example:\n\n- If we set $x=0$, we get the point\n$(x, y) = (0, 3(0) + 2) = (0, 2)$.\nThis point is indeed a solution to the system:\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3(0) - 2 &=-2 \\\\ 6(0) - 2(2) &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.$\nBoth equations are satisfied. Therefore, $(0,2)$ is a solution.\n- If $x= 1$, we get the point\n$(x, y) = (1, 3(1) + 2) = (1, 5)$.\nThis point is another solution to the system:\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3(1) - 5 &=-2 \\\\ 6(1) - 2(5) &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.$\nBoth equations are satisfied. Therefore, $(1,5)$ is a solution.\n\nBy selecting any $x$-value and finding the corresponding $y$-value, $(x,y)$ gives a solution to the system and, consequently, a point of intersection between the two lines." :tutorial/id #uuid "1b845842-4e57-5e13-88c4-5f2040525075" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9686 :tutorial/title "Understanding the Result"} :step/id #uuid "c5a54d4e-6187-547b-a529-49836ff17503" :step/math-academy-id 229018 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074713}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074713 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073778 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073482} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50351666-c691-510f-863c-168c61f9980b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050622}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542513} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542510}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System With Infinitely Many Intersection Points"} :step/id #uuid "f5487d1d-451a-5011-93d4-43de29b451fc" :step/math-academy-id 229019}] :activity/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073927}]}
